REGIONAL REGULATION OF OGAN KOMERING ULU SELATAN REGENCY

NUMBER 7 OF 2020

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF OGAN KOMERING ULU SELATAN,

Considering:

a. that the government has the obligation to provide protection, advancement, enforcement and fulfillment of Human Rights of every citizen including the Rights of Women and Children;
b. that Women and Children are vulnerable groups who tend to experience violence and discrimination and therefore need to receive optimal protection;
c. that the fulfillment of constitutional rights of Women and Children as well as the improvement of the quality of life of Women and Children, is one of the mandatory affairs which is the duty, authority and responsibility of the Regency Government;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Protection of Women and Children;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235), as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 237, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5946);
4. Law Number 37 of 2003 concerning Establishment of East Ogan Komering Ulu Regency, South Ogan Komering Ulu Regency and Ogan Ilir Regency in South Sumatra Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 152, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4347);
5. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
6. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
7. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as has been amended several times, most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
8. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
9. Government Regulation Number 59 of 2019 concerning Implementation of Coordination of Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2019 Number 160, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6382);
10. Regulation of the Minister of Women's Empowerment Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards in the Field of Integrated Services for Women and Children Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 483);
11. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 15 of 2010 concerning General Guidelines for Handling Children in Conflict with the Law (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 483);
12. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 903;

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF OGAN KOMERING ULU SELATAN REGENCY

and

REGENT OF OGAN KOMERING ULU SELATAN

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN.

Article 2

1. The implementation of Protection of Women is based on the principles of:
a. the best interests of Women;
b. gender justice and gender equality;
c. local wisdom; and
d. legal certainty.
2. The implementation of Protection of Children is based on the principles of:
a. non-discrimination;
b. the best interests of the Child;
c. the right to life, survival, and development;
d. respect for the views of the Child; and
e. legal certainty.

Article 3

The regulation of Protection of Women and Children aims to:
a. serve as a guideline for the Regency Government in planning, policies and strategies for the Protection of Women and Children;
b. protect Women and Children from actions, decisions and policies that violate the rights of Women and Children;
c. prevent violations of the Rights of Women and Children;
d. enhance empowerment of Women and Children;
e. enhance the role and value of local wisdom in the Protection of Women and Children; and
f. enhance the role of Regional Apparatus and non-Government entities in the Regency in efforts to Protect Women and Children.

Article 4

The scope of regulation of Protection of Women and Children includes:
a. prevention;
b. implementation of Protection of the Rights of Women and Children;
c. handling;
d. empowerment; and
e. guidance and supervision.

Article 5

Every Woman has the right to:
a. obtain protection from acts of discrimination;
b. obtain protection from economic exploitation and sexual exploitation;
c. obtain protection from physical, psychological, sexual violence, and neglect;
d. obtain decent work in accordance with her abilities;
e. obtain work leave while receiving wages/salary in accordance with laws and regulations;
f. obtain education and teaching in all types, levels and pathways of education;
g. obtain special protection in the performance of work or profession against matters that may threaten safety and/or health in connection with the reproductive function of Women;
h. choose, be chosen, be appointed in work, positions, and professions in accordance with the requirements and laws and regulations; and
i. ownership and management of joint property in marriage in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 6

1. Every Child has the right to:
a. every child has the right to protection by parents, family, community, and the state;
b. the rights of the child are human rights and for their benefit the rights of the child are recognized and protected by law even from the womb;
c. every child from the womb, has the right to live, survive, and improve their standard of living;
d. every child from birth, has the right to a name and citizenship status;
e. every child with physical and/or mental disabilities has the right to obtain care, education, training, and special assistance at state expense, to ensure their life in accordance with human dignity, enhance self-confidence, and the ability to participate in community, national, and state life;
f. every child has the right to worship according to their religion, think, express themselves in accordance with their intellectual level and age under the guidance of parents and/or guardians;
g. every child has the right to know who their parents are, to be raised, and cared for by their own parents;
h. in the event that the child's parents are unable to raise and care for their child properly, then the child may be cared for or adopted as a child by another person in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
i. every child has the right to be raised, maintained, cared for, educated, directed, and guided in their life by parents or guardians until adulthood in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
j. every child has the right to obtain adoptive parents or a guardian based on a court decision if both parents have died or due to a valid reason cannot carry out their obligations as parents;
k. adoptive parents or guardians as referred to in letter j must carry out the obligations as actual parents;
l. every child has the right to obtain legal protection from all forms of physical or mental violence, neglect, maltreatment, and sexual abuse while in the care of parents or guardians, or other parties responsible for the care of the child;
m. in the event that parents, guardians, or caregivers of a child commit any form of physical or mental abuse, neglect, maltreatment, and sexual abuse including rape, and or murder of a child who should be protected, then aggravated punishment must be imposed;
n. every child has the right not to be separated from their parents against the child's own will, unless there are valid legal reasons and rules showing that the separation is in the best interest of the child;
o. in the circumstances as referred to in letter n, the child's right to continue to meet directly and maintain personal contact regularly with their parents is still guaranteed by law;
p. every child has the right to obtain education and teaching in the context of personal development in accordance with their interests, talents, and level of intelligence;
q. every child has the right to seek, receive, and provide information in accordance with their intellectual level and age for their self-development as long as it is in accordance with moral values and propriety;
r. every child has the right to rest, associate with peers, play, recreate, and create in accordance with their interests, talents, and level of intelligence for their self-development;
s. every child has the right to obtain health services and social security appropriately, in accordance with their physical and spiritual mental needs;
t. every child has the right not to be involved in war events, armed conflicts, social unrest, and other events containing elements of violence;
u. every child has the right to obtain protection from economic exploitation activities and any work that endangers them, so that it can disrupt their education, physical health, morals, social life, and spiritual mentality;
v. every child has the right to obtain protection from sexual exploitation and abuse activities, kidnapping, child trafficking, as well as from various forms of narcotics, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
w. every child has the right not to be subjected to persecution, torture, or imposition of inhuman punishment;
x. every child has the right not to be deprived of their freedom unlawfully;
y. arrest, detention, or imprisonment of a child may only be carried out in accordance with applicable law and can only be implemented as a last resort;
z. every child who is deprived of their freedom has the right to be treated humanely and with attention to the needs of personal development according to their age and must be separated from adults, unless it is in their best interest;
aa. every child who is deprived of their freedom has the right to obtain legal assistance or other assistance effectively at every stage of applicable legal remedies;
bb. every child who is deprived of their freedom has the right to defend themselves and obtain justice before an objective and impartial Juvenile Court in a closed hearing to the public.
2. Every child who becomes a victim or perpetrator of sexual violence or who is in conflict with the law has the right to confidentiality.
3. Every Child with Disabilities in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in paragraph (1) is also entitled to obtain:
a. special education/special education;
b. rehabilitation;
c. social assistance; and
d. maintenance of social welfare standards.
5. Every Child who has excellence has the right to receive special education.

Article 7

1. The Regency Government in efforts to protect Women is obligated and responsible to:
a. guarantee Women's Rights without discrimination in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
b. establish and implement policy programs and activities for the protection of Women;
c. cooperate with the community and/or Community Organizations in the implementation of Women's Protection in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
d. carry out rehabilitation and empowerment of Women who are victims of acts of violence;
e. provide special protection to Women victims of acts of violence and human trafficking; and
f. take preventive action in order to prevent violations of Women's rights.
2. The responsibility of the Regency Government in efforts to protect Women's Rights as referred to in paragraph (1) is carried out by DPPKBP-PPPA.

Article 8

1. The Regency Government in efforts to protect Children is obliged and responsible to:
a. respect the fulfillment of Children's rights without distinguishing ethnicity, religion, race, class, gender, ethnicity, culture and language, legal status, birth order, and physical and/or mental condition;
b. provide facilities, infrastructure, and availability of human resources in the implementation of Child Protection;
c. guarantee the protection, care, and welfare of Children by taking into account the rights and obligations of Parents, Guardians, or other persons who are legally responsible for Children;
d. guarantee Children to convey their right to express opinions in accordance with the age and level of intelligence of the Child;
e. establish and implement policies and programs/activities for the protection of Children's rights;
f. cooperate with the community and/or Community Organizations in the implementation of Child Protection in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations;
g. carry out rehabilitation of Children who are victims of acts of violence;
h. provide special protection to Children:
1\. in emergency situations;
3\. in conflict with the law;
4\. from minority and isolated groups;
5\. who are exploited economically and/or sexually;
6\. who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
6\. who are victims of pornography;
7\. with HIV/AIDS;
8\. victims of kidnapping, sale, and/or trafficking;
9\. victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
10\. victims of sexual crimes;
11\. victims of terrorism networks;
12\. persons with disabilities;
13\. victims of mistreatment and neglect;
14\. with deviant social behavior; and
15\. who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
i. organize inclusive education for Children with special needs;
j. take preventive action in order to prevent violations against Children;  and
k. implement and support National policies in the implementation of Child Protection in the Regency carried out through efforts to realize Child-Friendly Regency, Child-Friendly Sub-district and Child-Friendly Village.
2. The responsibility of the Regency Government in efforts to protect Children as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented by DPPKBP-PPPA.

Article 11

The responsibility of the family in efforts to protect women includes:
a. protecting women from conflict and domestic violence that degrades the dignity of women;
b. providing equal opportunities for women to develop themselves in the fields of:
1\. education;
2\. social;
3\. economic; and
4\. politics and culture.
c. preventing violations of women's rights in the household.

Article 12

1. Parents in efforts to protect Children are responsible for:
a. caring for, maintaining, educating, and protecting Children;
b. developing Children according to their abilities, talents, and interests;
c. providing formal and non-formal education;
d. guiding Children to behave in accordance with religion, ethics and morals as well as customs; and
e. preventing marriage at the age of Children.
2. In the event that parents do not exist, or their whereabouts are unknown, or due to a reason, cannot carry out their obligations and responsibilities, then the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) may be transferred to the family.

Article 13

1. Efforts made by the Regency Government in order to prevent violations of the rights of Women include:
a. organizing socialization regarding the prevention of violence against Women by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. providing guidelines on handling acts of violence against Women;
c. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration;
d. developing an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against Women;
e. developing family empowerment programs;
f. strengthening the function of community organizations engaged in Women's empowerment; and
g. providing public facilities that are comfortable and safe for Women.
2. Efforts made by the Regency Government in order to prevent violations of Children's rights include:
a. preventing acts of violence against Children by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. developing child-friendly schools;
c. encouraging increased child participation through the establishment of Children's Forums;
d. providing guidelines on handling acts of violence against Children;
e. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration services for child victims of violence;
f. developing an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against Children;
g. strengthening the function of community organizations engaged in Child protection; and
h. encouraging the establishment of a Child-Friendly Regency.

Article 15

1. Efforts made by the family in order to prevent violations of Women's rights include:
a. protection of women from various violations of rights in the household; and
b. encouraging the formation of harmonious, educative and democratic families;
2. Efforts made by parents in order to prevent violations of Children's rights include:
a. providing good examples to children in accordance with religious and customary teachings;
b. supervision of children in their activities inside or outside the home;
c. providing opportunities for children to dialogue and express opinions democratically; and
d. protection of children from various acts of violation of Children's rights in the household.

Article 16

The Regency Government implements Protection of Women by involving:
a. family;
b. community leaders;
c. religious leaders;
d. teachers and/or academics;
e. the community; and
f. community organizations in the field of protection of Women.

Article 23

1. The Regency Government's efforts to provide protection of Children in the health sector include:
a. free health services for Children from poor families;
b. implementation of optimal health for Children to obtain good health status from the womb;
c. ensuring that infants receive exclusive breastfeeding from their mothers properly and naturally;
d. ensuring Children receive adequate nutrition;
e. ensuring newborn Children undergo screening; and
f. ensuring the provision of monitoring of growth and development of toddlers and pre-school age children.
2. The Regency Government, family, and parents protect children from efforts to transplant their body organs to other parties.

Article 32

1. The provisions as referred to in Article 31 may be exempted for Children aged between 13 (thirteen) years to 15 (fifteen) years, to perform light work as long as it does not interfere with physical, mental, and social development and health.
2. Employers who employ Children in light work as referred to in paragraph (1) must meet the requirements including:
a. written permission from parents or guardians;
b. work agreement between the employer and parents or guardians;
c. maximum working time of 3 (three) hours;
d. carried out during the day and does not interfere with school time;
e. occupational safety and health;
f. clear employment relationship; and
g. receive wages in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
3. The provisions as referred to in paragraph (2) letter a, letter b, letter f, and letter g are exempted for Children working in family businesses.

Article 35

1. Every person is prohibited from employing and involving Children in the worst forms of work.
2. The worst forms of work as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. all work in the form of slavery or similar practices;
b. all work that exploits, provides, or offers Children for:
1\. prostitution;
2\. pornography production;
3\. pornographic acts;
4\. gambling;
5\. production and trade of alcoholic beverages;
6\. narcotics;
7\. psychotropic substances; and
8\. other addictive substances; and/orc. all work that endangers the health, safety, or morals of Children.

Article 40

The implementation of handling Women and Children victims of violence includes:
a. handling complaints of victims of violence;
b. health services;
c. social rehabilitation;
d. law enforcement and legal assistance; and
e. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 41

1. The implementation of handling Women and Children victims of violence as referred to in Article 40 is carried out through integrated services.
2. Integrated services for Women and Children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) use the principles of:
a. gender responsive;
b. non-discrimination;
c. equal and respectful relations;
d. maintaining confidentiality;
e. providing a sense of security and comfort;
f. respecting individual differences;
g. non-judgmental;
h. respecting the choices and decisions of victims;
i. sensitive to the background and condition of victims and the use of appropriate language understood by victims;
j. fast and simple;
k. empathy;
l. fulfillment of the rights of Women and Children; and
m. free of charge.

Article 42

Services for handling complaints of victims of violence against Women and Children are carried out by:
a. hospitals;
b. community health centers;
c. P2TP2A;
d. Women and Children Service Unit (UPPA);
e. Trauma Center Shelter (RPTC);
f. Children's Social Shelter (RPSA);
g. Women Crisis Centre (WCC)h. Advisory Board for Marriage Guidance and Preservation (BP4);
i. Police;
j. Prosecutor's Office;
k. Court;
l. Legal Aid Institute (LBH);
m. Child Protection Institute (LPA);
n. Social Welfare Protection Institute; and
o. Shelters.

Article 44

1. Health services for Women and Children victims of violence include aspects of:
a. Promotive;
b. Preventive;
c. Curative;
d. rehabilitative.
3. Health services for Women and Children victims of violence are carried out:
a. Comprehensively;
b. accessible 24 (twenty-four) hours;
c. with quality; and
d. in accordance with health service standards.
3. Special health services for Children victims of violence must prioritize the best interests of the Child.
4. Women and Children victims of violence are entitled to health services, including:
a. medical services;
b. medicolegal services; and
c. psychosocial counseling.
5. Further provisions regarding procedures for implementing health services as referred to in paragraph (4) are regulated by Regional Regulation.

Article 45

1. Social rehabilitation services for Women and Children victims of violence are aimed at restoring and developing the abilities of victims who experience social dysfunction to be able to carry out their social functions in society properly.
2. Social rehabilitation services as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. counseling services;
b. provision of safe houses;
c. spiritual guidance.
3. The implementation of services as referred to in paragraph (2) is organized by:
a. Regional Apparatus that has duties and functions in the social field;
b. DPPKBPP-PA;
c. P2TP2A;
d. Women Crisis Centre (WeC);
e. community organizations in the social field; and/or
f. religious institutions.

Article 46

1. Counseling services as referred to in Article 45 paragraph (2) letter a aim to:
a. help victims recognize their problems and find effective ways to overcome them themselves;
b. empower victims to determine their future;
c. provide moral support for victims in facing the process they are going through; and
d. make victims feel accepted in the community environment.
2. Counseling services are carried out by gender-responsive social rehabilitation officers, consisting of:
a. counselors;
b. psychologists; and
c. psychiatrists.
3. Counseling services are carried out in places that guarantee a sense of security, comfort and confidentiality of information from victims.
4. The stages of counseling services include:
a. initial counseling;
b. follow-up counseling;
c. case conference; and
d. home visit.

Article 48

1. Every Woman and Child victim of violence is provided with spiritual guidance aimed at psychological recovery.
2. Spiritual guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented based on the principles of:a. not imposing any religion or belief; andb. leaving the final decision in the hands of the victim (self determination).
3. Spiritual guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) is conducted by spiritual guidance officers.
4. Spiritual guidance officers as referred to in paragraph (3) consist of:
a. consultants of the Marriage Advisory, Development and Preservation Agency (BP4);
b. Officers of the Office of Religious Affairs (KUA);
c. Ustad or Ulama;
d. Priests;
e. Pastors; and
f. other religious leaders who have gender sensitivity or gender awareness.

Article 50

1. Law enforcement and legal aid services as referred to in Article 49 are intended to provide assistance and protection to Women and Children victims of violence and witnesses with a gender-responsive approach so that victims obtain justice and legal certainty.
2. Types of legal aid services as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. legal aid in the reporting process and preparation of the Official Report;
b. legal aid in the prosecution process at the prosecutor's office;
c. legal aid in the examination process at court;
d. legal aid in the court hearing decision process until there is a final legal decision; and
e. legal aid in the mediation process.
3. The Regency Government provides legal aid to Children and Women victims of violence free of charge.
4. In providing legal aid as referred to in paragraph (3), the Regency Government may cooperate with:
a. Legal Aid Institutions (LBH);
b. Women Crisis Center (WCC);
c. Advocates; and/ord. other legal assistants who are gender sensitive.

Article 54

1. The Regency Government facilitates:
a. provision of business capital assistance for Women with weak economies;
b. marketing of products resulting from Women's businesses;
c. organization of training for community capacity development through economic empowerment of pre-prosperous families;
d. expansion of access, information and provision of knowledge to Women regarding marketing efforts for products produced; and
e. provision of comprehensive entrepreneurship training to Women.
2. The Regency Government in facilitating activities as referred to in paragraph (1) cooperates with the community and/or community organizations.

CHAPTER IX

CHILD-FRIENDLY REGENCY

Article 57

1. The Regency Government conducts guidance to Sub-district Governments in the development of a Child-Friendly Regency.
2. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. coordination;
b. facilitation;
c. guidance;
d. supervision;
e. consultation; and
f. education and training.
3. Provisions regarding Child-Friendly Regency are further regulated by Regional Regulation.
